---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DarkHeader from '../../components/DarkHeader.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Trading mpg for clearance — Cooper Minehart"
  description="40 stock vehicles scored on off-road geometry and plotted against EPA fuel economy. Which ones nobody beats on both."
>
  <DarkHeader>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-10">
      <a href="/hobbies" class="text-sm text-forest-400 hover:text-forest-300 mb-6 inline-block">← Hobbies</a>
      <p class="section-label-light mb-3">Hobbies · Trail / Range</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Trading mpg for clearance</h1>
      <p class="text-stone-300 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        How much of a mileage hit am I willing to take to feel like I can go off-road
      </p>
    </div>
  </DarkHeader>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-10 pb-24 text-[#1a1a1a]">
    <div class="space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#333]">
      <p>
        I've got car-brain lately. I see a Tacoma parked in my neighborhood and I think 'that would move my friends and skis and bikes pretty well'. I hate car-brain. The lust takes over and distract me from enjoying my every day. This month though I'm weak and I've submitted to car-brain. Claude and I made a little tool to compare off-road and efficiency tradeoffs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now... no one is going to like how I've quantified off-road here. It's a combo of ground clearance, approach/departure/breakover angles, and tire diameter (each normalized and you can modify the weight of each). You can toggle on low range, lockers, and EVs. If you really hate it, the code is <a href="https://github.com/cminehar-coder/trail-range" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 hover:underline">here</a> and you can rebuild it with your own value-judgements. MPG is just EPA combined.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="tr-fig" data-tr-fig="field">
      <div class="tr-chips"></div>
      <div class="tr-wrap"><svg role="img" aria-label="Off-road score vs EPA combined MPG, gas and hybrid vehicles"></svg><div class="tr-tip" hidden></div></div>

    </figure>

    <div class="space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#333]">
      <p>
        At the extremes we have the Bronco Raptor (15mpg, though Walt's gonna be mad cause it's so wide) and the RAV4 Hybrid (39 mpg). Based on our indisputably correct off-road score, the frontier is linear at lower MPG and progressively concave at higher efficiency. Hmmm... I want to say -- this suggests you can buy the Raptor, Sasquatch, Rubicon, or Tacoma with rough indifference if your utility between off-road-score and mpg is linear, though if you're willing to drive a RAV4 then it or the Bronco Raptor become the only options -- but that's putting more trust in the off-road-score than I think we should.
      </p>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] pt-4">
        Add EVs and switch to miles-per-dollar
      </h2>
      <p>
        Electricity and gas costs are here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="tr-fig" data-tr-fig="ev">
      <div class="tr-chips"></div>
      <div class="tr-wrap"><svg role="img" aria-label="Off-road score vs miles per dollar of energy, all 40 vehicles"></svg><div class="tr-tip" hidden></div></div>
      <figcaption class="tr-live"></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#333]">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] pt-4">
        Move the weights, whatever, idk I don't really go offroad
      </h2>
      <p>
        If you think Claude didn't approximate your off-road-utlity-function very well, change how we weight the different features here. I don't know, I don't really go off road. I just want to be able to drive up a dirt road and not have my Prius swallowed by Pittsburghs potholes again.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="tr-fig" data-tr-fig="weights">
      <div class="tr-chips"></div>
      <div class="tr-wrap"><svg role="img" aria-label="Frontier under different score weights"></svg><div class="tr-tip" hidden></div></div>

    </figure>

    <div class="space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#333]">
      <p>
        We're not looking at traction, sexiness, articulation, gearing, reliability, MSRP, or lots of other stuff. EPA combined is a lab number and manufacturers measure clearance differently. 40 configurations (2010-2027) is a curated list, not every trim.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white shadow-sm">
        <iframe
          src="/maps/trail-range/index.html"
          title="Trail / Range off-road vs fuel economy explorer"
          class="w-full block"
          style="height: 90vh; min-height: 760px; border: 0;"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-stone-200 text-sm text-[#555] leading-relaxed">
      <p>Coop</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script is:inline src="/maps/trail-range/model.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/maps/trail-range/data.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/maps/trail-range/post-charts.js"></script>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  .tr-fig { margin: 1.75rem 0 2rem; }
  .tr-chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4rem; margin-bottom: .6rem; }
  .tr-chip { font-size: 12.5px; padding: .3rem .7rem; border: 1px solid #d6d3cc; border-radius: 999px; background: #fff; color: #444; cursor: pointer; }
  .tr-chip[aria-pressed="true"] { background: #172d30; border-color: #172d30; color: #fff; }
  .tr-wrap { position: relative; border: 1px solid #e7e5e0; border-radius: 8px; background: #fffefa; }
  .tr-wrap svg { display: block; width: 100%; }
  .tr-ax { font-size: 11px; fill: #626e6c; }
  .tr-axt { font-size: 11.5px; fill: #172d30; }
  .tr-lab { fill: #626e6c; }
  .tr-on { fill: #172d30; font-weight: 600; }
  .tr-pt { cursor: pointer; }
  .tr-tip { position: absolute; pointer-events: none; background: #172d30; color: #fff; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.4; padding: .45rem .6rem; border-radius: 6px; max-width: 220px; z-index: 5; }
  .tr-live { font-size: 12px; color: #888; margin-top: .5rem; line-height: 1.5; }
</style>
